ons L26:   4. cite16†3 Experiments L27:     1. cite17†3.1 Experiment 1: Positional Selection (Copy-Back Task) L28:       1. cite18†Setup. L29:       2. cite19†Results. L30:     2. cite20†3.2 Experiment 2: Content-Based Selection (Key-Value Retrieval) L31:       1. cite21†Setup. L32:       2. cite22†Results. L33:     3. cite23†3.3 Experiment 3: WikiText-2 Language Modeling L34:       1. cite24†Setup. L35:       2. cite25†Results. L36:       3. cite26†Connecting to the $\\mathcal{O}(\\log N)$ prediction. L37:     4. cite27†3.4 Experiment 4: WikiText-103 Language Modeling L38:       1. cite28†Setup. L39:       2. cite29†Results. L40:     5. cite30†3.5 Experiment 5: Post-Training Compression of GPT-2 L41:       1. cite31†Setup. L42:       2. cite32†Results. L43:       3. cite33†Deployment via factored keys. L44:       4. cite34†Interpretation. L45:       5. cite35†Recovery via QK fine-tuning. L46:     6. cite36†3.6 Experiment 6: Architecture Generalization (LLaMA 125M) L47:       1. cite37†Motivation. L48:       2. cite38†Setup. L49:       3. cite39†Results. L50:     7. cite40†3.7 Experiment 7: SVD + Fine-tuning at Scale (Mistral 7B) L51:       1. cite41†Motivation. L52:       2. cite42†Setup. L53:       3. cite43†Results. L54:   5. cite44†4 Analysis L55:     1. cite45†4.1 Scaling of Minimum $d_{\\text{select}}$ L56:     2. cite46†4.2 Overfitting Masks the Effect L57:     3. cite47†4.3 Practical Benefit: KV Cache, Not Parameters L58:   6. cite48†5 Related Work L59:     1. cite49†Multi-Query and Grouped-Query Attention. L60:     2. cite50†Low-Rank Attention. L61:     3. cite51†Efficient Attention. L62:     4. cite52†Compression and Pruning. L63:   7. cite53†6 Discussion L64:     1. cite54†Limitations. L65:     2. cite55†Interaction with Flash Attention. L66:     3. cite56†Composability with KV cache quantization. L67:     4. cite57†Implications for architecture design. L68:     5. cite58†Practical adoption path. L69:   8. cite59†7 Conclusion L70:   9. cite60†References L71: cite61†License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0†info.arxiv.org L72: \nL73: arXiv:2603.04427v1 [cs.LG] 16 Feb 2026\nL74: # Thin Keys, Full Values:\nL75: Reducing KV Cache via Low-Dimensional Attention Selection\nL76: \nL77: Hengshuai Yao Affiliation: Sapient Intelligence Affiliation: Department of Computing Science, University of Alberta Email: hengshu1@ualberta.ca    Guan Wang Affiliation: Sapient Intelligence Email: one@sapient.inc\nL78: ###### Abstract\nL79: Standard transformer attention uses identical dimensionality for queries, keys, and values ($d_{q}=d_{k}=d_{v}=d_{\\text{model}}$). Our insight is that these components serve fundamentally different roles, and this symmetry is unnecessary. Queries and keys produce scalar attention weights (selection), while values carry rich semantic representations (value transfer).\nL80: We argue that selection is an inherently lower-dimensional operation than value transfer, requiring only $\\mathcal{O}(\\log N)$ dimensions to distinguish among $N$ relevant patterns.\nL81: We validate this hypothesis across seven experiments: (1) positional selection tasks requiring just 1 dimension per head, (2) content-based retrieval requiring $\\sim\\!\\log_{2}N$ dimensions, (3–4) WikiText-2 and WikiText-103 language modeling where $d_{\\text{select}}=d_{\\text{model}}/4$ incurs only 4.3% perplexity increase while reducing QK parameters by 75%, (5) post-training SVD compression of GPT-2, revealing keys to be far more compressible than queries, with lightweight QK fine-tuning recovering nearly all quality loss, (6) a 125M-parameter LLaMA model confirming identical degradation ratios across architectures, and (7) Mistral-7B (7.2B parameters), where SVD compression followed by QK fine-tuning achieves 75% key cache savings at just 2.0% residual quality cost.\nL82: For existing models, SVD compression followed by QK fine-tuning (3 epochs on a small fraction of pretraining data) achieves 75% key cache savings at $<$2% residual quality cost. For a 7B-parameter model serving 128K context, asymmetric attention saves 25 GB of KV cache per user, enabling approximately 60% more concurrent users on the same GPU.\nL83: ## 1 Introduction\nL84: \nL85: The self-attention mechanism (cite62†Vaswani et al., 2017 ) computes:\nL86: \nL87:  | $$\\text{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\\text{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{QK^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_{k}}}\\right)V$$  |  | (1)\nL88: where $Q=XW_{Q}$, $K=XW_{K}$, and $V=XW_{V}$ are linear projections of the input sequence $X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d_{\\text{model}}}$. In virtually all modern transformer architectures—GPT (cite63†Radford et al., 2018 ), BERT (cite64†Devlin et al., 2019 ), LLaMA (cite65†Touvron et al., 2023 )—the projection dimensionality is identical: $d_{q}=d_{k}=d_{v}=d_{\\text{model}}$.\nL89: \nL90: This symmetry is a design convention, not a necessity. Attention performs two functionally distinct operations:\nL91: \nL92:   1. 1.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversal priors: solving empirical Bayes via Bayesian inference and pretraining (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.15136v1)\nciteturn31948view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.15136v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1025\n"
